WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Minor external bleeding typically comes from small scrapes, shallow cuts, or abrasions

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.020
that only affect the top layers of your skin.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:12.400
Because this type of bleeding is slow, your goal is to get it under control quickly by

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.440
using that keyword we just talked about, pressure.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.660
First step is to apply firm pressure to the wound.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.120
If the person is alert, you can actually ask them to hold the pressure themselves while

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.760
you take a moment to get your supplies and put on your gloves.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.639
Once the bleeding slows down, you'll want to wash the area thoroughly with soap and

00:00:31.639 --> 00:00:32.639
water.

00:00:32.639 --> 00:00:36.320
If it is permitted in your setting and the bleeding has stopped, you can apply a bit

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.119
of antibiotic ointment and then cover the wound with a clean dressing or a simple adhesive

00:00:41.119 --> 00:00:42.119
bandage.

00:00:42.119 --> 00:00:46.340
Advise the person to follow up with a healthcare provider if the wound is gaping and might

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.959
need stitches, or if they were injured by a dirty object, which could mean they need

00:00:50.959 --> 00:00:52.160
a tetanus shot.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.680
Now, let's put those materials we passed out to use.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.119
I want everyone to start by putting on your gloves right now.

00:00:59.119 --> 00:01:03.759
Once you have them on, take your first gauze square and place it firmly over the injury

00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:06.000
to apply initial pressure.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.559
Once in place, never pull off that initial layer of gauze as it can dislodge the clots

00:01:10.559 --> 00:01:12.959
that are starting to form and interrupt the pressure.

